  ),
  (
    'd1000001-0000-4000-8000-100100000039',
    'a1000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000001',
    '{"passage":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wind power is the use of wind energy to generate useful work. Historically, wind power was used by sails, windmills and windpumps, but today it is mostly used to generate electricity. This article deals only with wind power for electricity generation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Today, wind power is generated almost completely using wind turbines, generally grouped into wind farms and connected to the electrical grid. In 2025, wind supplied about 2,700 TWh of electricity, which was over 8% of world electricity. With about 100 GW added during 2021, mostly in China and the United States, global installed wind power capacity exceeded 800 GW."}]}]},"keywords":[{"id":"kw1","text":"power"},{"id":"kw2","text":"electricity"},{"id":"kw3","text":"States"},{"id":"kw4","text":"United"}],"difficulty":"medium","source":{"provider":"wikipedia","title":"Wind power","pageid":277289,"revision_id":1362144136,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_power","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","retrieved_at":"2026-07-02T11:41:21.820Z"}}'::jsonb,
    true,
    'approved',
    NOW()
  ),
  (
    'd1000001-0000-4000-8000-10010000003a',
    'a1000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000001',
    '{"passage":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An electric vehicle (EV) is a vehicle propelled mostly by electric power. EVs encompass road (cars, buses, trucks and personal transporters), rail (trains, trams and monorails), boats and submersibles, aircraft (fixed-wing and multirotors) and spacecraft. EVs originated in the late 19th century."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Electricity was among the preferred methods for powering early motor vehicles because it was quieter, provided comfort, and ease of operation. However, its limited range hindered mass adoption throughout the 20th century."}]}]},"keywords":[{"id":"kw1","text":"vehicle"},{"id":"kw2","text":"electric"},{"id":"kw3","text":"20th"},{"id":"kw4","text":"19th"}],"difficulty":"medium","source":{"provider":"wikipedia","title":"Electric vehicle","pageid":279350,"revision_id":1361754248,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_vehicle","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","retrieved_at":"2026-07-02T11:41:21.820Z"}}'::jsonb,
    true,
    'approved',
    NOW()
  ),
  (
    'd1000001-0000-4000-8000-10010000003b',
    'a1000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000001',
    '{"passage":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Solar power, also known as solar electricity, is the conversion of energy from sunlight into electricity, either directly using photovoltaics (PV) or indirectly using concentrated solar power. Solar panels use the photovoltaic effect to convert light into an electric current."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Concentrated solar power systems use lenses or mirrors and solar tracking systems to focus a large area of sunlight to a hot spot, often to drive a steam turbine."}]}]},"keywords":[{"id":"kw1","text":"power"},{"id":"kw2","text":"Solar"}],"difficulty":"easy","source":{"provider":"wikipedia","title":"Solar power","pageid":13690575,"revision_id":1362144562,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_power","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","retrieved_at":"2026-07-02T11:41:21.820Z"}}'::jsonb,
    true,
    'approved',
